Check the pull request head against each acceptance line below, in order. Run every command from the repository root at the PR head. Run `node --version` first and record it. To compare output exactly, including the single trailing newline, pipe the program's output to `od -c` (or `cmp` it against a file written with `printf`) as well as reading it.

Check 1. Line: "`printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0."
Run: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo "exit=$?"`, and also `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c`.
PASS: stdout is exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by one newline, and then `exit=0`.
FAIL: any other stdout (different text, a missing newline, or extra content) or a nonzero exit status.

Check 2. Line: "With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]` followed by a newline."
Run: `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c`, and also without `od`.
PASS: stdout is exactly `[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]` followed by one newline.
FAIL: any other stdout.

Check 3. Line: "With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]` followed by a newline."
Run: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c`, and also without `od`.
PASS: stdout is exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]` followed by one newline.
FAIL: any other stdout, including rows missing or out of order.

Check 4. Line: "Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline."
Run: `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | od -c`, and also without `od`.
PASS: stdout is exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by one newline.
FAIL: any other stdout.

Check 5. Line: "Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a\tb":"1\t2"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output."
Run: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | od -c`, and also without `od`.
PASS: stdout is exactly the JSON text `[{"a\tb":"1\t2"}]`, where each `\t` is the two-character JSON escape (backslash followed by `t`), followed by one newline.
FAIL: any other stdout, for example `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]`.

Check 6. Line: "`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output."
Run: `npm test; echo "exit=$?"`. Then run `grep -rn -- '--tsv' test/` and read each matching test.
PASS: `npm test` ends with `exit=0`, and at least one test in test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with the `--tsv` argument on input that contains tab characters and asserts on the output it prints.
FAIL: `npm test` exits nonzero, or no test in test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with `--tsv` on tab-separated input and checks its output. A test that only calls parse() directly, or never asserts on the output, does not count.

Check 7. Line: "README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation."
Read README.md at the head.
PASS: README.md mentions the `--tsv` flag and gives an example command line that uses it, such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`.
FAIL: README.md does not mention `--tsv`, or mentions it without any example invocation.

